<?xml version='1.0' standalone='yes'?>

<!DOCTYPE PLUGIN [
<!ENTITY name          "btrfs-integrity-recovery">
<!ENTITY author        "iiLaurens">
<!ENTITY version       "2026.08.02">
<!ENTITY bundleversion "2026.08.02">
<!ENTITY launch        "Settings/btrfs-integrity-recovery">
<!ENTITY pluginURL     "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/iiLaurens/unraid-btrfs-integrity-recovery/main/plugin/btrfs-integrity-recovery.plg">
<!ENTITY bundleURL     "https://github.com/iiLaurens/unraid-btrfs-integrity-recovery/releases/latest/download/btrfs-integrity-recovery-x86_64-1.txz">
]>

<PLUGIN name="&name;" author="&author;" version="&version;" launch="&launch;" pluginURL="&pluginURL;">

<CHANGES>
##&name;

###2026.08.02
- Live status endpoint: scrub-rs now serves real-time error + progress
  counters on a localhost-only HTTP port (STATUS_PORT, default 9101; set 0 to
  disable). The Settings page shows a live "Status" panel that polls it every
  2 s while a scrub runs, instead of just "running / idle".
- Coarse scrub progress: `progress_pct` (float, two decimals, monotonic
  non-decreasing) on the status endpoint — the fraction of data dev-extents
  scrubbed so far, known up front with no full scan. 1 TiB+ disks get ~1000+
  steps (~0.1% resolution); tiny test images show chunky ~10% jumps.
- New `scrub.sh status` subcommand (curls the live server) backing the panel.
- Internal refactor: filesystem re-confirmation moved behind the `fs` seam
  (filesystem-owned `Reconfirmer`), the array canary moved to a glue module,
  and the recovery batch writer no longer depends on btrfs internals. No
  user-visible behaviour change.

###2026.08.01b
- Settings form: align inputs with their pre-text labels (fixed-width label
  column, no more whitespace gap on wide screens); removed the redundant
  "Current: ..." hint behind the schedule dropdown.

###2026.08.01a
- Settings page no longer overflows the viewport: the settings table is
  constrained to the page width and long log lines scroll inside the log
  block instead of widening the page.
- Removed the redundant post-input hint labels (the field name + help bubble
  already describe each option).

###2026.08.01
- Log management simplified: past runs are rotated (newest 3 kept), selectable
  and viewable verbatim from the Settings page; the status JSON file, progress
  and current-device tracking were removed.
- Running state is now derived live from a run lock (atomic mkdir), which also
  prevents a manual and a scheduled scrub from running simultaneously for the
  same disk(s). Stale locks are reclaimed automatically.
- Notifications now fire in real time: "Scrub started" when each disk's scrub
  launches, "Scrub finished" (with severity based on the result) as soon as
  each disk completes, and "Scrub stopped" on manual stop.
- Fixed: multi-disk DEVICES and custom CRON values in the config file were
  unquoted and broke when sourced by bash; they are now quoted and valid for
  both the PHP and bash config parsers.
- Added scrub.sh running|devices subcommands; removed status|lastlog.
</CHANGES>


<!--
Install the bundle. It already contains the prebuilt binaries
(scrub-rs, craft-corrupt) plus all plugin files, so there is NO
binary download at install time (we build & ship it ourselves).
-->
<FILE Name="/boot/config/plugins/btrfs-integrity-recovery/install/btrfs-integrity-recovery-x86_64-1.txz" Run="upgradepkg --install-new">
<URL>&bundleURL;</URL>
</FILE>

<!-- Run install logic (binaries are already in place from the bundle). -->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash" Method="install">
<INLINE>/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/btrfs-integrity-recovery/scripts/install.sh</INLINE>
</FILE>

<!-- Re-run the same logic on upgrade: upgradepkg re-extracts the bundle,
     but without this Method="upgrade" hook the post-install steps
     (perms, default config, rc restart) would be skipped on version bumps. -->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash" Method="upgrade">
<INLINE>/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/btrfs-integrity-recovery/scripts/install.sh</INLINE>
</FILE>

<!-- Uninstall logic. -->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash" Method="remove">
<INLINE>/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/btrfs-integrity-recovery/scripts/uninstall.sh</INLINE>
</FILE>

</PLUGIN>
